{"kind":"assert","text":"R3 SOURCE-ONLY design on the current authority-run-bound v2 draft; no edits, parser, runtime, install or commit. This concerns ordinary authority refusal, not acceptance or the held 4096 run-key proposal.\n\nCurrent seams: scope/core.ts595-610 reads standing before receiverReads/fetchFacts but checks only saturation; 629 reads further observations; 632 enters Turns. prepare723-739 likewise enters Turns after standing. turn.ts239-245 invokes Waiting.refuse before #drain and on each resumed section. That hook currently checks only saturation. judge.ts328-357 implements the actual alternative: qualifying current grant OR exact grantByRule fallback. authority.ts held returns [] for a value that refuses; null means observation unavailable, not unauthorized. Recovery-key membership.ts627-630 reads the local roster and actor and can pass with []/null; founding-key614-616, by-invitation640-650 and register founding-policy293-301 establish other local authority paths. Do not deny on [] alone or on action===null.\n\nRecommended minimal staged implementation:\n1. Extract the ordinary grant matching and mark alternative from derive judge check9 into a shared pure authority decision, used by final judge and refusal-only precheck. Keep exact key/action/covers/notAfter and membership semantics; preserve null=\u003eauthority-unavailable, not unauthorized. No new name allowlist and no copy of recovery policy in Core.\n2. Plain string grants can establish unauthorized without fetching any named fact: there is no rule alternative. For a grant mark, construct its real local check7/8 RuleGiven using shared prefix resolution, and call the SAME grantByRule. Current membership recovery rows have local typed fields/roster and are reachable this way. Never call a general mark with fabricated empty facts/subjects or missing observations/values and interpret false as denial. If that prefix depends on unavailable external inputs or cannot be established, return explicit defer. Fact-dependent rules stay on the existing path; this is a stated boundary, not proof of universal early refusal. Action-null/public/local founding/invitation routes retain existing behavior unless their real complete local context is established.\n3. Run the refusal-only decision synchronously immediately after #standing settles and before receiverReads/fetchFacts/Observes.before. Use CURRENT scope/head, clockOf(view, clock.read()), and the original held observation. Re-read accepted(actor,key) now: any accepted key, including one accepted during the await, bypasses this new guard so original accepted/mismatch processing remains authoritative.\n4. Re-run the same live decision at Turns.refuse, before drain and each resume, using current local state/clock. Do not capture a denied/allowed boolean or phase-one grant as final admission. Existing hook may capture store/clock synchronously or take them explicitly. Preserve saturation priority and the final head comparison, full judge, observation/highest-head/window/clock checks, and sealing/told. A preliminary pass is never a grant or receipt.\n5. Preparations need the corresponding shared grant check including covers and notAfter (Core granted callback alone does not perform those final checks; derive/prepare232-237 does). Check prepared(intentDigest, capability, step) at the CURRENT view before refusing; do not shadow repeat receipts. Recompute trusted step needs on current local context and retain exact action/window/scopeGrant binding; if it changed from the held read, defer/unavailable, never unauthorized for a different action. Invalid/unknown step and needs-null retain original judgment. scopeGrant continues to come only from the trusted declared step table.\n\nConstraints: Existing judgeAct deliberately returns due before check9 (judge.ts227-230); calling full judgeAct unchanged cannot be an early guard. Calling it with fabricated facts can stop at dependency-unavailable (241-258), hiding the question rather than proving denial. A dedicated shared authority checkpoint is necessary; do not reorder the final commit judge or run effects/derive as a preflight. Serving early unauthorized also intentionally precedes foreign-field errors; that response-order choice should be named. Null/unavailable authority is a separate case, not justification for unauthorized. A guard that finds authorization at its current head cannot promise timed work will never change it; final judgment still handles that.\n\nMinimal meaningful witnesses: (a) genuine scope with a due transition and a well-shaped foreign-fact-bearing act by a denied signer: unauthorized, no receiver/fact/further read, no timed entry/operation/head/alarm movement; independent alarm still drains. (b) real membership recovery-key fallback with empty ownStanding grant succeeds, while an unrelated key refuses; action-null founding/invitation/register open-policy paths and a normal current-grant path remain accepted. (c) gate an existing rule preparation, concurrently obtain a real revoked observation/head advance, make timed work due, then release: pre-resume guard refuses before drain; a stale allowed boolean must fail this witness. (d) accept same key while phase one waits, then release: exact retry and differing-intent mismatch remain answered from history without new foreign work; include preparation repeat. (e) narrow derive control whose grant mark genuinely depends on a foreign fact: precheck defers, actual fetched fact still determines final authority. Use gates, not sleeps.\n\nSource-only limits: no change or runtime proof; the generic fact-dependent exception needs explicit scope in the implementation/report. Existing sticky-limit preparation path currently lacks the act's early accepted-key bypass; preserve repeat semantics explicitly when adding ordinary checks rather than assuming all current saturation behavior is correct."}